intent:
I manage our office candy drawer, and the obvious move is to buy whichever familiar chocolate listing has the highest displayed rating. The frozen candidates are a $9.99 Kinder Bueno five-pack rated 98 percent over eight reviews, a $22.00 personalized twelve-bar Hershey milk-chocolate pack rated 100 percent over two reviews, a $36.95 Godiva-branded variety set packed by Candy Cabin rated 100 percent over one review, and a $14.99 two-pound Goldenberg dark-chocolate peanut-chew assortment rated 95 percent over twelve reviews. The Goldenberg page says it has the same quality and taste always known, while people often claim that famous brands quietly change recipes, replace cocoa butter with cheaper fats, or shrink packages. What do these ratings actually justify, does this frozen evidence prove any of those historical claims, and what should I stock?

Please audit the exact offers and available background under these boundaries:

- keep each title SKU price rating review count pack count mass flavor and manufacturer or repacker statement at frozen seller assertion scope;
- carry every displayed rating with its review count and do not treat 100 percent over one or two reviews as equal to a larger sample;
- do not treat the Goldenberg same quality and taste always known copy as a historical recipe or taste comparison;
- preserve the Godiva pages ambiguous mass carton and mini bar wording and its Candy Cabin identity field;
- do not infer current ingredients cocoa butter substitution formula version or applicable legal category from brand or title alone;
- state chocolate naming and fat rules by market and jurisdiction and do not present the frozen background as current legal advice;
- require dated before and after records for the same SKU or a documented version mapping before calling recipe change quality downgrade or shrinkflation;
- compare net quantity pack count and total paid on a common unit basis while preserving product form and market;
- keep community statements at their author category product time storage and preference scope not as exact chocolate proof;
- verify current physical identity ingredients allergens net quantity manufacturer or repacker market lot and delivered cost before purchase;
- screen office allergies dietary rules and shared food handling before any tasting or stocking;
- use only a small reversible brand masked trial with equal portions matched temperature and randomized or counterbalanced order;
- predeclare acceptance consumption repeat choice and waste thresholds; and
- choose only the lowest total cost exact offer that passes current label allergy budget and office trial gates otherwise use an accepted control or defer.

Give me a practical research and stocking process that will:

1. build an exact offer matrix for price rating review count pack count mass product form and identity fields;
2. separate jurisdiction specific chocolate label and fat rules from exact SKU classification;
3. specify the dated same SKU before after evidence needed for recipe change and shrinkflation claims;
4. reconcile quality decline rating disagreement and temperature anecdotes without cross category transfer;
5. verify current physical packages ingredients allergens net quantity identity market lot delivered cost and any historical counterparts;
6. calculate comparable unit and accepted serving costs only after pack and product form normalization;
7. run a small matched office trial with brand masking and predeclared local outcomes; and
8. choose the lowest total cost exact offer passing all current label safety budget and local trial gates or use an accepted control or defer.

Explain what each seller page, rating denominator, chocolate-type or labeling rule, unit-price method, and community report can and cannot establish. For any recipe-change or shrinkflation claim, spell out the matched historical records still needed instead of filling gaps with brand folklore. End with a reversible office-stocking procedure that I could actually follow, and keep any result local to the tested office, product versions, market, lots, and serving conditions rather than declaring a universal brand winner.
